const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does shipping from China to Finland take?',
    a: 'Allow roughly 35–45 days total by sea — 32–40 days from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to a northern-range hub (Rotterdam, Hamburg or Gdańsk) plus a ~3–5-day Baltic feeder to Helsinki or Kotka (MEDIUM confidence, planning estimate). A rail hybrid via Hamburg/Duisburg plus short-sea runs roughly 22–28 days, and no verified Finnish air transit is published, so request that from your carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in Finland?',
    a: '25.5% standard — raised from 24% on 1 September 2024 — with reduced rates of 14% (food, restaurants) and 10% (books, medicines, passenger transport). Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so 5% duty plus 25.5% VAT compounds to ×1.31775, not 30.5% — a $10,000 CIF shipment at 5% duty lands at $13,177.50 before destination fees.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to Finland?',
    a: 'Helsinki (UN/LOCODE FIHEL, Vuosaari terminal) is the capital gate and largest container port, while Kotka (FIKTK, part of Port of HaminaKotka) is Finland\u2019s largest general-cargo port for the eastern Gulf of Finland. Both are feeder ports reached by transshipping at Rotterdam, Hamburg or Gdańsk — direct China deep-sea calls to Finnish ports are rare.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the Baltic freeze affect shipping to Finland?',
    a: 'Yes — the Gulf of Finland freezes roughly December–April, so winter sailings need ice-class vessels (1A/1A Super) and Finnish icebreaker assistance, and Finland charges higher fairway dues in winter. That is a seasonal schedule and cost variable a western EU port like Rotterdam or Hamburg never carries.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship China-to-Finland by rail?',
    a: 'Not as a direct lane — Finland runs 1524mm broad gauge against Europe\u2019s 1435mm standard gauge, and the historic rail route through Russia is sanctions-restricted. So \u201crail to Finland\u201d in practice means rail to a Baltic hub (Gdańsk, Hamburg) plus a short-sea ferry across the Baltic — roughly 22–28 days total, making Finland a sea-versus-air market like Ireland.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is Finland\u2019s currency not a landed-cost risk (unlike Sweden)?',
    a: 'Finland is in the eurozone, so you pay both ocean freight and import VAT in EUR. Sweden\u2019s krona floats freely and Denmark\u2019s krone is pegged at ±2.25% under ERM II, so Finland is the only Nordic market on this list that carries zero Nordic currency risk.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does Finland\u2019s landed cost compare with Sweden\u2019s?',
    a: 'On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty, Finland\u2019s 25.5% VAT lands at $13,177.50 versus Sweden\u2019s 25% at $13,125 — a $52.50 gap, the half-point VAT margin — and $682.50 above Germany\u2019s 19% at $12,495, before the Baltic feeder and destination fees are added.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI to import into Finland?',
    a: 'Yes — one EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU, and it must exist before the goods arrive; zero Finnish imports clear without it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Finland?',
    a: 'FCL is priced per container (northern-range benchmark 20ft $1,200–$4,800, 40ft $1,600–$6,800; Finland-specific figures are not published and must be requested) and LCL per cubic metre ($60–$160/CBM), with the true break-even between 7.5 and 20 CBM — at the top of the LCL range, 15 CBM already costs $2,400, which is $1,200 above the 20ft FCL floor.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for Finnish customs clearance?',
    a: 'The core set is four documents plus one classification — commercial invoice, bill of lading, packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC code, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products add CE marking evidence and chemicals add REACH registration.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I defer VAT when importing into Finland?',
    a: 'Finland, like other EU states, allows EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) to defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are CE marking and REACH, and do they apply in Finland?',
    a: 'CE marking is the EU conformity mark required for regulated products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices — and REACH is the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Finland exactly as in every member state.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do SABER or SASO apply to Finland?',
    a: 'No — SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems and apply to zero Finnish or EU imports. Finland uses CE marking and REACH instead, so do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Finnish quote.',
  },
];

