        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Finland is entered through two Baltic feeder ports — Helsinki (FIHEL) and Kotka (FIKTK) — neither of which takes direct China deep-sea calls, so a <strong>~3–5-day feeder</strong> from Rotterdam, Hamburg or Gdańsk sits on top of the <strong>32–40-day</strong> northern-range ocean leg, for roughly <strong>35–45 days</strong> total. The statutory math compounds: 5% duty + 25.5% VAT = 1.05 × 1.255 = <strong>×1.31775</strong>, so a $10,000 CIF becomes <strong>$13,177.50</strong> — <strong>$52.50 higher</strong> than Sweden's 25% on the same base. There is no practical direct China rail lane because Finland runs <strong>1524mm broad gauge</strong> and the Russia route is sanctions-restricted, so "rail" is really rail-plus-short-sea in <strong>22–28 days</strong>. In winter the Gulf of Finland freezes <strong>Dec–Apr</strong>, adding ice-class vessels and icebreaker/fairway dues no western EU port pays. Finland is also the only eurozone Nordic here — <strong>EUR</strong>, no SEK/DKK currency risk.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Finland's FCL/LCL
