  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Finland deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Finland is the Nordic test of three structural variables that most guides flatten into "a 25.5%
      VAT country" — the <strong>gauge</strong>, the <strong>ice</strong> and the <strong>euro</strong>.
      The country data shows Helsinki as the capital, EUR as the currency (not the SEK or DKK of its
      neighbours) and a <strong>25.5% standard VAT</strong> layered on the <strong>EU Common Customs
      Tariff</strong>, a duty line that is uniform across every member state and varies only by HS code,
      typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first gate is geography plus the port tier. Helsinki (FIHEL) and Kotka (FIKTK) are
      <strong>feeder ports, not deep-sea mainline calls</strong> — a China vessel rarely sails direct to
      the Gulf of Finland, so cargo transships at Rotterdam, Hamburg or (increasingly) Gdańsk and feeds
      north through the Danish straits. That Baltic leg adds <strong>~3–5 days</strong> and a feeder
      surcharge the northern-range ocean quote silently omits, and it is exactly the leg where Finland's
      winter bites.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finland changes the equation a second way: it runs <strong>1524mm Russian broad gauge</strong>
      against Europe's 1435mm standard gauge, and its historic rail lane to China runs through Russia —
      now sanctions-restricted. The result is that Finland, like Ireland, has <strong>no practical direct
      China–Europe rail lane</strong>, so "rail to Finland" is really rail-to-a-Baltic-hub plus a short-sea
      ferry. On the commercial side, the honest position is that <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published
      only as a northern-range Rotterdam reference</strong> (MEDIUM confidence), while the Baltic feeder,
      rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not — so those lines stay explicit request-for-quote
      markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Indicative freight rates from China to Finland</h2>
      <p>
        Finland inherits the published <strong>northern-range Rotterdam benchmark</strong> from the
        WorldFreightHub Europe route data, and it is important to read those figures for what they are —
        a corridor reference that <em>stops before</em> the Baltic feeder. The snapshot carries
        <strong>$1,200–$4,800 for a 20ft</strong>, <strong>$1,600–$6,800 for a 40ft</strong> and
        <strong>$60–$160 per CBM for LCL</strong>, all MEDIUM confidence. The <strong>Baltic feeder
        surcharge</strong> to Helsinki/Kotka, and the rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures, are
        <strong>not published</strong> — so those stay LOW with a request-for-quote marker rather than
        borrowing a neighbouring Baltic figure.
      </p>

