  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Finland clock has two beats for ocean cargo: the <strong>northern-range sea leg</strong>,
      then the <strong>Baltic feeder leg</strong>. The WorldFreightHub Europe route data puts sea transit
      at roughly <strong>32–40 days via the Cape of Good Hope</strong> from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to
      Rotterdam or Hamburg, then a <strong>~3–5-day feeder</strong> to Helsinki or Kotka — a planning total
      of roughly <strong>35–45 days</strong>. Direct China calls to Finnish ports are rare, so transshipment
      is the norm. Rail from Xi'an or Chengdu runs <strong>18–22 days</strong> to Hamburg or Duisburg and
      then continues by short-sea/ferry to Finland in a planning total of roughly <strong>22–28 days</strong>
      — there is no direct rail lane because of the 1524mm gauge break. Finnish air transit is not published,
      so that line stays explicit instead of borrowing a neighbouring airport figure.
    </p>

