      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Critical point — the rail premium does not pay for itself on financing alone:</strong>
        the rail-plus-short-sea hybrid saves roughly 7–23 days (22–28 versus 35–45 sea) at roughly 2–3× the
        sea rate. At an 8% cost of capital a $50,000 container costs ≈ $11/day in transit, so ~15 days is
        only about <strong>$165 of financing saved</strong> — which alone does not pay the rail premium.
        The hybrid is justified instead by <strong>stockout avoidance and market timing</strong> on
        high-value or seasonal cargo, and it is not a direct train anyway — Finland's 1524mm gauge means
        the last leg is always a ferry or feeder. Air is the extreme-time option, with no verified Finnish
        rate or transit published, so request that as an itemised quote rather than assuming a neighbouring
        airport figure.
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose sea when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Cost per kg matters more than the ~35–45-day sea-plus-feeder window.</li>
            <li>The cargo is heavy, palletised or container-sized and can plan ahead.</li>
            <li>You can accept the Baltic feeder leg and its extra fee.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose rail-plus-short-sea when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Stockouts or seasonal windows are the risk and you need ~22–28 days instead of ~35–45.</li>
            <li>You accept roughly 2–3× sea cost plus the short-sea leg for ~7–23 days saved.</li>
            <li>The final ferry/feeder move is acceptable to your destination.</li>
