  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges that competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Finland's statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on CIF</strong>
        (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>25.5% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. Below
        that line sit the charges that are unique to Finland — the <strong>~3–5-day Baltic feeder leg</strong>
        and the <strong>winter ice/fairway dues</strong> — plus the operational charges the verified snapshot
        does not quantify: THC, documentation, inspection, port storage, demurrage and detention. This is
        where a cheap freight quote becomes an expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.255.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf's 5%. For an illustrative
        USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500 duty-inclusive
        base, then 25.5% VAT of USD 2,677.50, giving USD 13,177.50 before the Baltic feeder and destination
        fees. The compound is <strong>1.05 × 1.255 = ×1.31775</strong>, not the 30.5% you would get by
        simply adding the two headline rates. Rates and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current
        duty line and VAT treatment with Finnish Customs before relying on this example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The Rotterdam northern-range reference, EU duty and 25.5% VAT are MEDIUM/HIGH confidence from route/customs sources. Unquantified Finnish, feeder and port fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — landed cost & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">What most guides skip</h3>
      <p>
        Five lines change the Finnish landed cost and rarely appear on a standard ocean quote:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>25.5% VAT is newer and higher than most guides still cite.</strong> Finland raised its
          standard VAT from 24% to 25.5% on 1 September 2024, which puts it a half-point above Sweden's and
          Denmark's 25% — $52.50 more on a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty, and the EU's highest standard tier
          except Hungary's 27%.</li>
        <li><strong>The Baltic feeder leg is not in the northern-range quote.</strong> A Rotterdam or Hamburg
          figure ends at the quay; the ~3–5-day feeder to Helsinki/Kotka and its surcharge is a separate
          charge that must be added before the quote is usable.</li>
        <li><strong>The winter ice season is a real cost and schedule variable.</strong> The Gulf of Finland
          freezes roughly December–April, so winter sailings need ice-class vessels (1A/1A Super), Finnish
          icebreaker assistance and higher fairway dues — a seasonal line Rotterdam or Hamburg never pays.</li>
        <li><strong>1524mm gauge means there is no direct rail lane.</strong> Finland's broad gauge breaks
          with Europe's 1435mm standard, and the Russia rail route is sanctions-restricted, so "rail to
          Finland" is rail-plus-short-sea — effectively a sea move in disguise.</li>
        <li><strong>EUR removes the currency risk its neighbours carry.</strong> Finland is the only eurozone
          Nordic on this list, so there is no SEK/DKK exposure on the duty-inclusive value — a variable
          Swedish and Danish importers must hedge and Finnish importers simply ignore.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage vs detention: two clocks, two payees</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the charge the <strong>terminal</strong> applies when import cargo
        stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is the
        charge the <strong>ocean carrier</strong> (or feeder operator) applies when the importer keeps the
        container beyond the equipment free time after collection. They sound similar, but they are
        separate contracts, separate free-time allowances and separate per-day rates — and in a
        feeder-heavy route like Finland the detention clock can also run against the feeder carrier as
        well as the mainline line, on top of any ice-related delays in winter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finnish and Helsinki/Kotka free-time periods and per-day amounts are not published in the verified
        snapshot, so do not assume a Rotterdam or Hamburg schedule applies. Before booking, ask the carrier
        and the forwarder to put the Helsinki/Kotka free time, the inland free time, demurrage rate and
        detention rate in writing. The most reliable avoidance tactic is documentary readiness: register the
        EORI early, lock the TARIC code, prepare the invoice/packing list/B-L and file the Finnish
        declaration promptly so release happens before either free-time clock runs out.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Hidden charges to ask for on the quote</h3>
      <p>
        Request an itemised schedule that separates each destination line. A quote that only says "freight
        + customs" can hide <strong>the Baltic feeder surcharge</strong>, <strong>THC</strong> at
        Helsinki/Kotka, a <strong>documentation fee</strong>, a possible <strong>customs inspection fee</strong>,
        <strong>port storage</strong>, <strong>winter ice/fairway dues</strong>, and the
        <strong>demurrage/detention</strong> exposure if release is delayed. Without those lines, a dollar-level
        freight quote cannot be compared or used for landed-cost budgeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: Finland duty, 25.5% VAT, EORI &amp; conformity</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      Finland applies the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, so duty is identical across member
      states and depends on the HS code — typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong>, with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force. Import <strong>25.5% VAT</strong> (14% and 10% reduced on
      some goods) is then charged on the <strong>duty-inclusive value</strong>, so the baseline statutory
      calculation is <code>(CIF + duty) × 1.255</code>. Specific goods can still carry different duty lines
      or VAT treatment, so the HS code remains the first compliance decision.
    </p>

    <h3>EORI — register before arrival</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>EORI number</strong> is mandatory for any import into the EU and must be registered
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for
      China-to-Finland cargo, so start the registration before the vessel departs China, not while the
      feeder is discharging at Helsinki or Kotka.
    </p>

    <h3>TARIC classification and documents</h3>
    <p>
      Finland classifies goods under the <strong>10-digit TARIC code</strong>, which drives the duty
      line, VAT treatment, permits and restricted screening. The standard document set is a commercial
      invoice, bill of lading, packing list and the correct TARIC classification, backed by your EORI
      number. Confirm the current declaration workflow with a licensed Finnish broker.
    </p>

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated
      products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others. <strong>REACH</strong> is
      the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Finland
      exactly as in every member state, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file
      before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>VAT deferral via EU customs warehousing</h3>
    <p>
      Finland, like other EU states, allows <strong>EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63)</strong> to
      defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for
      importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU. Given Finland's 25.5% VAT tier, the deferral
      is worth more cash than in a 19% or 20% market, so ask your broker about the procedure before
      assuming VAT must be paid at the quay.
    </p>

    <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
    <p>
      SABER and SASO are <strong>Saudi-only</strong> conformity systems and do not apply to Finland or the
      EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Finnish quote. Finland uses CE marking and REACH
      instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with Finnish customs or your broker.
    </p>

    <h3>De-minimis and IOSS</h3>
    <p>
      The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro
      on most consignments. The <strong>€150</strong> figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the
      <strong>IOSS</strong> scheme simplifies VAT on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the
      current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with Finnish customs before relying on
      these figures.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Finland customs, duty, VAT & conformity" />
  </section>

