  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>At a 5% duty assumption, a $10,000 CIF shipment clears at $13,177.50: $500 duty (5% × 10,000), $2,677.50 VAT on the $10,500 duty-inclusive base (25.5% × 10,500), a 31.775% uplift (1.05 × 1.255 = 1.31775) — $52.50 above Sweden's 25% and $682.50 above Germany's 19% on the same base.</li>
      <li>Finland's 25.5% VAT on CIF + duty equals 26.775% of CIF (25.5% × 1.05), so it adds $127.50 more than flat 25.5% on $10,000 CIF (2,677.50 − 2,550) — and it is the EU's highest standard tier except Hungary's 27%, a half-point above Sweden's and Denmark's 25%.</li>
      <li>The rail-plus-short-sea hybrid at 22–28 days saves 7–23 days versus the ~35–45-day sea-plus-feeder window (35 − 28 = 7; 45 − 22 = 23) at roughly 2–3× the sea rate — but Finland's 1524mm gauge means the last leg is always a ferry or feeder, never a through train.</li>
      <li>The 15 CBM heuristic is fragile: at LCL $60–160/CBM, 15 CBM costs $900–2,400 (15 × 60 = 900; 15 × 160 = 2,400), already $1,200 above the $1,200 20ft FCL floor at the top of the LCL range — the true crossover sits at 7.5–20 CBM (1,200 ÷ 160 = 7.5; 1,200 ÷ 60 = 20).</li>
      <li>Helsinki's ≈0.5m TEU is 0.5 ÷ 13.4 = 3.7% of Rotterdam's ≈13.4m TEU, and both Finnish ports are feeders reached by a ~3–5-day Baltic leg — so the feeder surcharge plus the winter ice season, not the northern-range rate, decide the Finnish landed cost.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, Finland is the most structurally expensive Nordic route to price wrong: the Baltic feeder plus the winter ice season are the real landed-cost drivers, not the northern-range rate. I would not treat "rail to Finland" as a rail lane unless the 1524mm-gauge short-sea transfer is quoted separately.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Neither Helsinki nor Kotka takes direct China deep-sea calls, so the 32–40-day ocean leg plus a ~3–5-day Baltic feeder becomes 35–45 days — and Helsinki's ≈0.5m TEU is only 3.7% of Rotterdam's ≈13.4m TEU.</li>
      <li>The Gulf of Finland freezes roughly December–April, adding ice-class vessels (1A/1A Super), icebreaker assistance and fairway dues that western EU ports never pay.</li>
      <li>The 25.5% VAT stack compounds to ×1.31775 ($13,177.50 on a $10,000 CIF), $52.50 above Sweden's 25% — while 1524mm broad gauge means the 22–28-day "rail" option is really rail-plus-short-sea, never a through train.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><em>Disclosure: This is the author's editorial judgment based on the published data on this page — not an independently verified fact.</em></p>
  </section>

